    <h1>How to Separate Gold From <em>Black Sand</em></h1>
    <p>Four steps, done in order: classify the concentrate to one size range, work the density difference in water, lift the magnetics out dry, then finish the flour gold by hand. Skip the order and gold walks out with the sand.</p>
    <p style="font-size:0.85rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,0.5);margin-top:16px">Published {{ datePublished | readableDate }} · Gold Wash Plants</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="container">
    <article class="content">
      <p>Black sand is what sits in the bottom of the pan after the light material washes away, and it is what builds up at the end of a sluice run. It is mostly magnetite and other heavy iron minerals. Gold ends up mixed into it for one simple reason: both are heavier than everything else in the gravel, so anything that traps one traps the other.</p>

      <p>Separating the two is not one trick. It is a sequence, and the order matters more than the tools. Classify by size, use water to work the density difference, take the magnetic fraction out dry, then clean up what is left. Most of the gold people lose at cleanup is lost by doing step two before step one.</p>

      <h2>What black sand actually is</h2>

      <p>The black grains are usually magnetite, often with hematite, ilmenite, chromite or garnet mixed in. None of it is gold. It follows gold around because it shares the one property that placer recovery depends on, which is weight. Any riffle, mat or pan that holds a piece of gold will also hold a grain of magnetite.</p>

      <p>That is worth sitting with, because miners sometimes treat black sand in the cleanup bucket as a problem with the plant. It is closer to the opposite. A concentrate with no black sand in it usually means the recovery system never grabbed the heavy fraction in the first place. Black sand showing up is evidence the sluice is working.</p>

      <h2>Why gold gets locked in it</h2>

      <p>Gold is far heavier than magnetite, and magnetite is heavier than ordinary quartz sand. When every particle in the bucket is roughly the same size, that difference does the separating for you and water is all you need.</p>

      <p>The trouble starts when the gold is much smaller than the sand around it. At the fine end, weight stops being the force in charge. A flake of flour gold is held by surface tension and by the film of water clinging to it, so it can float on the surface of a pan and travel out with material a fraction of its density. This is the same problem covered in our guide to <a href="/blog/fine-gold-recovery/">fine gold recovery</a>. Our plants are built to hold gold down to 200 mesh, which is 0.075mm, and the sluice design is the reason.</p>

      <h2>Step one: classify before you separate</h2>

      <p>Run the concentrate through a nested set of screens and split it into size fractions before you try to separate anything. Most prospectors work with something like a coarse screen, a middle screen around 20 mesh, and a fine screen at 50 or 100 mesh, then handle each pile on its own.</p>

      <p>Trying to clean a single bucket holding everything from pea gravel down to dust is the most common way gold gets lost at the cleanup table. Coarse sand needs more water and more agitation to move, and that same water carries the fine gold from the smaller fraction straight off the edge. Separate the sizes and each pile behaves predictably.</p>

      <h2>Step two: work the density difference in water</h2>

      <p>With the concentrate sorted by size, water and gravity do most of the job. A finish pan worked slowly and patiently will do it. A blue bowl run at low flow will do it with less skill required, and it handles the fine fractions well. A miller table, which is a wet vibrating table with a fine sheet surface, is the tool most miners reach for on the finest material because you can watch the gold line up and take it off deliberately.</p>

      <p>Whatever the tool, the rule is the same: less water than you think, and a slower feed than you think. Speed is what pushes fine gold out. Work one size fraction at a time and stop when the tails run clean rather than when the bucket is empty.</p>

      <h2>Step three: pull the magnetics out dry</h2>

      <p>Dry the concentrate completely before you go near it with a magnet. A strong magnet inside a plastic bag or a magnetic wand, passed over the spread-out concentrate, lifts the magnetite out cleanly. Release it into a separate container and pass the pile two or three more times.</p>

      <p>Wet black sand is the mistake here. Damp grains clump, and the clumps carry fine gold along with them into the discard pile. Dry it first, always, and keep what the magnet pulled until you have checked it.</p>

      <p>One more thing that catches people: not all black sand is magnetic. Hematite, chromite and garnet will not come to a magnet at all. A magnet alone never finishes the job, which is why it belongs in the middle of the sequence and not at the end of it.</p>

      <h2>Step four: the last of the flour gold</h2>

      <p>After the magnetics are gone you are usually left with a small amount of non-magnetic heavy minerals and the gold. A careful finish pan, a blue bowl at the lowest flow it will hold, or a miller table will take it from here. Working under good light with a snuffer bottle and a loupe is not overkill at this stage, because the pieces you are chasing are the ones that took the most effort to catch.</p>

      <p>Some miners stop before this point and sell the concentrate as it is rather than spend the hours. That is a reasonable call on a high volume operation, and it is worth pricing both ways before deciding.</p>

      <p>Mercury is the one method worth naming in order to rule out. It is restricted or banned in many jurisdictions and the health risk is serious and well documented. Every plant we build is mercury-free by design, and the recovery numbers below are achieved without it.</p>

      <h2>The bigger number is the gold you never saw</h2>

      <p>Everything above is cleanup. It works on gold your plant already caught, and no amount of table work recovers what left in the tailings.</p>

      <p>That is where the real money sits. Our wash plants are built for a 95-98% recovery rate including fine gold down to 200 mesh, and <a href="/how-it-works/">how the wash and sluice stages work</a> explains where in the plant that happens. If you are hauling out a lot of black sand and finding very little gold in it, the thing to examine is upstream: feed rate, water volume across the sluice, riffle spacing and the condition of the matting. Our <a href="/blog/gold-wash-plant-maintenance-tips/">maintenance guide</a> lists the checks in order.</p>

      <p>Sticky ground is its own case. Clay holds fine gold in suspension and carries it through the sluice bound up in lumps that never break apart, which no cleanup method can fix afterwards. That problem is handled at the wash deck, and it is covered in <a href="/blog/processing-clay-bound-gold/">processing clay-bound gold</a>.</p>

