      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <h3>Is there always gold in black sand?</h3>
      <p>No. Black sand is mostly magnetite and other heavy iron minerals, and it collects in the same places gold collects because it is heavy, not because it contains gold. Plenty of black sand carries no gold at all. What black sand does tell you is that your pan, sluice or plant is holding the heavy fraction, which is exactly where gold would be if the ground has any.</p>

      <h3>Can you separate gold from black sand without mercury?</h3>
      <p>Yes. Screening the concentrate to one size range, working the density difference in water with a pan, blue bowl or miller table, and then lifting the magnetic fraction out dry with a magnet will separate gold from black sand without any chemistry. Mercury is restricted or banned in many places and carries a real health risk. Our equipment is mercury-free by design.</p>

      <h3>Why is fine gold so hard to get out of black sand?</h3>
      <p>Because at very small sizes, weight stops being the dominant force. A flake of flour gold is held by surface tension and the film of water around it, so it can ride on top of water and travel with much lighter material. Screening the concentrate so gold and sand are in the same size range restores the density difference, and it is the step most miners skip.</p>
